@meta
id: 05-15
title: Replacing one word in a chain
chapter: 05
type: lesson
skills: word-e, WORD-motions
par: 21
minutes: 6
vscode: double-click the method name and type over it
open: order.provider.ts

@brief
The VS Code instinct for renaming one piece of `this.orderRepository.findFirst`
is to double-click it. The double-click is doing two jobs at once: it finds
the boundaries of the word, and it deletes what is inside them.

You have not been given a delete command yet — that is chapter eight. But
you already have both halves of this in pieces: `e` finds the end of a
word, and `Ctrl-W` in insert mode rubs out the word behind the cursor,
which you learned in chapter three.

Put them together. `findFirst` returns a nullable record and the codebase
uses `findOne` everywhere else. Change it.

Do not retype the rest of the chain — if your fix touches
`orderRepository`, you did it the slow way and par will say so.

@keys
| `9e` | nine word-ends forward |
| `a` | append after the cursor |
| `Ctrl-W` | in insert mode: delete the word before the cursor |
| `Esc` | out |

@hints
Get to the *end* of `findFirst`, then start typing there. Something you
already know deletes backwards a word at a time once you are in insert
mode.
--
`Ctrl-W` in insert mode deletes back to the start of the word — and it
uses the same word rule `e` does, so on `orderRepository.findFirst` it
stops at the dot and takes only `findFirst`.
--
`2j` then `9e` puts you on the last `t` of `findFirst`: the stops are
`const`, `order`, `=`, `await`, `this`, `.`, `orderRepository`, `.`,
`findFirst`. Then `a`, `Ctrl-W`, type `findOne`, `Esc`, `:wq`.

@solution
    2j  9e  a  <Ctrl-W> findOne <Esc>  :wq<Enter>

`Ctrl-W` stopping at the dot is the point. It respects the same boundary
`w` and `e` do, so one press takes exactly the identifier you were
standing at the end of and nothing else — the double-click, without the
mouse and without the selection to lose.

Two things follow from that. First, `ea` then `Ctrl-W` is a complete
rename-this-token move that works today, with nothing from a later
chapter. Second, if you wanted the whole `this.orderRepository.findFirst`
gone instead, `Ctrl-W` would need four presses, because it is word-sized
and not WORD-sized. There is no capital version of it.

Chapter eight replaces this with `cw`, which is two keys and does not
involve counting to nine. Learn this shape anyway: the reflex of landing
on a boundary before you start typing is the part that carries over.

@start order.provider.ts
export class OrderProvider {
  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findFirst({ where: { id } });

    return order;
  }
}

@expected order.provider.ts
export class OrderProvider {
  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    return order;
  }
}
